353
00:31:36,096 --> 00:31:38,477
[SPEAKER_00]: But not knowing that, you put it there.

354
00:31:39,017 --> 00:31:40,878
[SPEAKER_00]: That's why you're responsible for its existence.

355
00:31:40,978 --> 00:31:45,940
[SPEAKER_00]: That's why you're responsible for anything that happens in the time of that fully assumed existence.

356
00:31:46,460 --> 00:31:50,022
[SPEAKER_00]: But again, it's important to recognize that that's your starting point.

357
00:31:51,402 --> 00:31:55,464
[SPEAKER_00]: Because if you don't recognize that as a starting point, then you will say, oh, this is not real.

358
00:31:56,051 --> 00:31:58,053
[SPEAKER_00]: The reality of things is really pure.

359
00:31:58,593 --> 00:32:03,717
[SPEAKER_00]: The reality of things is nibbāna, and our ignorance is just delusion, which is not real.

360
00:32:04,597 --> 00:32:11,843
[SPEAKER_00]: That would be a true statement if there was the discernible beginning for ignorance.

361
00:32:11,863 --> 00:32:15,706
[SPEAKER_00]: If there was a first point, before which things are not ignorant, then develop ignorance, I can say.

362
00:32:15,946 --> 00:32:16,486
[SPEAKER_00]: But it's not.

363
00:32:17,127 --> 00:32:25,713
[SPEAKER_00]: Whenever you look, however you look, whatever you look, you're going to look with your ignorance, which means that is your starting point, that mistake.

364
00:32:26,882 --> 00:32:31,424
[SPEAKER_00]: But because it's a mistake, because it's not supposed to be there, that's why arahantship is possible.

365
00:32:33,065 --> 00:32:44,671
[SPEAKER_00]: So, nibbāna is present in everyone's experience as possibility, as a possibility, but not as the ultimate reality, uh, that you sort of achieve just by denying your mistake.

366
00:32:45,912 --> 00:32:51,595
[SPEAKER_00]: Because you deny your mistake, and that's a mistake, through your mistake, through your ignorance.

367
00:32:51,615 --> 00:32:54,997
[SPEAKER_01]: So, the beginning one star with one center self is, in a sense,

368
00:32:55,913 --> 00:32:57,353
[SPEAKER_01]: Starting with one's ignorance.

369
00:32:57,673 --> 00:32:58,474
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

370
00:32:58,774 --> 00:33:04,555
[SPEAKER_00]: And again, it's not that you find a way out of ignorance, left or right from ignorance, or above or below.

371
00:33:04,575 --> 00:33:07,036
[SPEAKER_00]: No, it's right through, while ignorance is still present.

372
00:33:07,456 --> 00:33:10,216
[SPEAKER_00]: The recognition of I am, sense of self.

373
00:33:10,416 --> 00:33:12,277
[SPEAKER_00]: And then, through that, you can abandon it.

374
00:33:14,297 --> 00:33:25,520
[SPEAKER_01]: One thing I was, I guess you'd say, excited about, and I'll set the right terms, I had always kind of not quite understood what saṅkhara, pañca, viññāṇa meant.

375
00:33:27,678 --> 00:33:30,680
[SPEAKER_01]: Like, oh, like, oh, yeah, because there's a termination, there's consciousness.

376
00:33:31,061 --> 00:33:31,421
[SPEAKER_01]: Right.

377
00:33:31,441 --> 00:33:31,821
[SPEAKER_01]: Whatever.

378
00:33:32,381 --> 00:33:39,966
[SPEAKER_01]: But I kind of had an idea that I think connect... hopefully connected something I wanted to read.

379
00:33:40,126 --> 00:33:41,047
[SPEAKER_01]: Can I read the passage?

380
00:33:41,067 --> 00:33:41,407
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

381
00:33:42,928 --> 00:33:50,313
[SPEAKER_01]: So, I wrote viññāṇa being the fundamental determination in that things must be present before anything else.

382
00:33:52,306 --> 00:33:56,349
[SPEAKER_01]: Depends on the fact that there is determination at all.

383
00:33:57,070 --> 00:34:02,554
[SPEAKER_01]: Or the the term I made was like determinationism.

384
00:34:03,515 --> 00:34:05,277
[SPEAKER_01]: Not like determinism.

385
00:34:05,297 --> 00:34:10,001
[SPEAKER_00]: How would you explain or rather how would you describe then the nature of determinationism?

386
00:34:10,261 --> 00:34:11,102
[SPEAKER_00]: How would you describe it?

387
00:34:11,842 --> 00:34:15,746
[SPEAKER_00]: What's the most fundamental sort of nature of it?

388
00:34:15,766 --> 00:34:18,208
[SPEAKER_00]: What's in common to every determinationism?

389
00:34:20,072 --> 00:34:25,975
[SPEAKER_00]: In Determinationism, there's to be two things present.

390
00:34:26,155 --> 00:34:26,595
[SPEAKER_00]: Exactly.

391
00:34:27,115 --> 00:34:28,936
[SPEAKER_00]: So, it cannot be by itself.

392
00:34:29,776 --> 00:34:33,258
[SPEAKER_00]: And if it cannot be one, just me, it cannot be self.

393
00:34:33,858 --> 00:34:35,359
[SPEAKER_00]: Because that's what the nature of self is.

394
00:34:36,339 --> 00:34:43,443
[SPEAKER_00]: So, that's why it's because there are already two, means that nature of determination is there.

395
00:34:43,463 --> 00:34:45,003
[SPEAKER_00]: Because there has to be.

396
00:34:46,064 --> 00:34:48,705
[SPEAKER_00]: There have to be two things simultaneously present.

397
00:34:49,462 --> 00:34:55,126
[SPEAKER_00]: And consciousness, being the most fundamental one of them, means it requires the other one, which is matter.

398
00:34:55,906 --> 00:35:05,973
[SPEAKER_00]: So, because consciousness cannot stay stand-alone, that's why it's sort of subordinate to the nature of determination, the nature of having two things bound together.

399
00:35:07,054 --> 00:35:08,595
[SPEAKER_00]: And none of them can be the first.

400
00:35:09,435 --> 00:35:11,436
[SPEAKER_00]: Hence, saṅkhara, pañca, viññāṇa.

401
00:35:11,757 --> 00:35:14,919
[SPEAKER_00]: That's how consciousness originates, as defined consciousness.

402
00:35:15,419 --> 00:35:16,840
[SPEAKER_00]: Because it's not the other thing.

403
00:35:17,890 --> 00:35:21,531
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, that's basically the conclusion that I got to.

404
00:35:21,791 --> 00:35:30,053
[SPEAKER_01]: Like, the fact that there is determinationism, okay, where it just works for me, is why consciousness can be at all.

405
00:35:30,193 --> 00:35:31,313
[SPEAKER_00]: Exactly, yeah.

406
00:35:31,333 --> 00:35:34,094
[SPEAKER_01]: Otherwise, it just would be, well, entirely incongruentable.

407
00:35:34,454 --> 00:35:36,154
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, like... You couldn't look at it.

408
00:35:36,174 --> 00:35:37,855
[SPEAKER_01]: Purely positive, what does that all mean?

409
00:35:37,875 --> 00:35:40,375
[SPEAKER_00]: You couldn't reflect onto it, if there's only one thing present.

410
00:35:40,455 --> 00:35:43,796
[SPEAKER_00]: There's no reference point for it.

411
00:35:43,976 --> 00:35:47,037
[SPEAKER_00]: So, that's why even the nature of the self

412
00:35:47,886 --> 00:35:48,726
[SPEAKER_00]: It's in its heart.

413
00:35:48,747 --> 00:35:49,987
[SPEAKER_00]: It's an absolute contradiction.

414
00:35:50,647 --> 00:35:52,268
[SPEAKER_00]: There can't be one.

415
00:35:52,288 --> 00:35:52,829
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

416
00:35:53,409 --> 00:35:59,172
[SPEAKER_01]: He wrote once, like, perceptions of unity, so to say, are what keep the self alive.

417
00:35:59,192 --> 00:35:59,652
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, exactly.

418
00:35:59,912 --> 00:36:04,555
[SPEAKER_00]: And you can't possibly conceive unity without having diversity.

419
00:36:05,195 --> 00:36:06,215
[SPEAKER_00]: That's then united.

420
00:36:06,455 --> 00:36:06,756
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

421
00:36:07,196 --> 00:36:12,399
[SPEAKER_00]: But then your point of view cannot be united with it, because if you were, you wouldn't be aware of it.

422
00:36:12,619 --> 00:36:15,140
[SPEAKER_00]: So consciousness is that sort of

423
00:36:16,219 --> 00:36:26,266
[SPEAKER_00]: Nature, just presence of consciousness and the non-consciousness, the two simultaneous things, means there's always a point of view, which means you cannot be that thing you're having a view of.

424
00:36:27,246 --> 00:36:30,628
[SPEAKER_00]: So, that's why even if you witness the unity, you are not part of it.

425
00:36:31,989 --> 00:36:35,231
[SPEAKER_00]: So, if you truly become part of unity, it means you become completely unaware.

426
00:36:35,752 --> 00:36:38,293
[SPEAKER_00]: But then, what kind of notion of unity is that?

427
00:36:39,354 --> 00:36:45,398
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you realize any notions of unity that you can have, even in your imagination, will be fully based upon diversity.

428
00:36:46,240 --> 00:36:49,681
[SPEAKER_01]: Some mystics might say pure unawareness is something to look for.

429
00:36:49,761 --> 00:36:58,323
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, but even that, they can develop that notion only based on diversity, which means, well, it can't be than pure unity, because it's diversified.

430
00:36:59,384 --> 00:37:01,364
[SPEAKER_00]: So, it's just, it's complete contradiction.

431
00:37:01,444 --> 00:37:03,305
[SPEAKER_00]: We can never be so surmounted by that.

432
00:37:06,806 --> 00:37:12,187
[SPEAKER_01]: So, and I was quite happy to start getting an inkling of what that actually referred to.

433
00:37:12,667 --> 00:37:13,207
[SPEAKER_00]: That was cool.

434
00:37:13,247 --> 00:37:14,848
[SPEAKER_00]: You know, it is quite important, because

435
00:37:16,923 --> 00:37:26,308
[SPEAKER_00]: Most of people, no matter, you know, some people might press it far enough in terms of the kind of detailed understanding, some people might not go into so much detail, but it doesn't matter.

436
00:37:26,768 --> 00:37:38,695
